Five revise, eleven clear, no reject.

## 1–2. Opening block

**1 · cfo** and **2 · competitor** (clear vs revise on "The owners and the calendar below are not
yet approved; no owner is recorded") — **clear, both.** The sentence states the record truthfully,
and the page now opens the door the version 2 critique asked for ("Approve the owners and the
calendar — Kamen Kamenov before Month 1"). The weakness both revisers point at was closed by
round 2; nothing in the line widens a claim or hides a cost.

## 3. Advocacy Enablement Push

**3 · cfo** (revise vs clear on the targets and KPI lines) — **clear.** Every KPI item names its
measure in this card's own words ("post quality scored on a 1-5 Advocacy Enablement Post Rubric
for proof link, human responsibility, stop-line clarity and required disclosure"), and the targets
say what is read and when (Month 11). A funder can govern it.

## 4. The always-on loop

**4 · journalist** (clear vs revise, the reviser citing the toolkit's "Employees and advocates /
Employee advocacy push") — **clear.** The loop now deploys "the trained advocates" and names them
from the card that equips them. The card's name drifted from the toolkit's ("Advocacy Enablement
Push" for "Employee advocacy push"); that is a producer-fidelity point the upstream-trace seats
judged clear on this unit, not a journalist's weakness in the loop sentence.

## 5–7. The proof-building order

Both upstream-trace seats agreed this paragraph needs revision (recorded defect): it states UP's
generic order (cases, then thought leadership, then visibility) and never relates it to the
one-pager's practical order for this engagement ("public identity cleanup, proof library lock,
message testing, founder-led proof launch and earned validation").

**5 · buyer-read** and **7 · employee-insider** (revise vs clear) — **revise, both.** The paragraph
is one dense sentence with no month and no card name, so neither a buyer nor an insider can check
it against the calendar on the same page. Repair: each stage names the cards and months that carry
it, in the one-pager's practical order.

**6 · cfo** (clear vs revise) — **clear.** No cost or commitment is claimed in the paragraph; the
funder's reading is unaffected by its sequencing.

## 8. The rollout

**8 · upstream-trace** (revise vs clear, the reviser citing the same one-pager line) — **clear.**
"First audience: owners of midsize businesses, especially Elena; then widen after the proof base is
live" is the one-pager's own first audience. The cited producer line is about sequence, not
audience, and the rollout does not contradict it.

## 9–11. Ownership, cadence and dependencies

Both employee-insider seats agreed on a defect here (recorded): the doors name roles, not people —
"United Partners project lead must assign it", "The B Team delivery owner must assign it". A door
that a role must open is not a door anyone can walk through, and half the roles are UP's, assigning
the client's owners.

**9 · buyer-read** (clear vs revise on the Public Identity Cleanup row) — **revise.** Same
weakness: the assigner must be a named person the page already knows — the platform decider Kamen
Kamenov, or a named owner from another row — not a role.

**10 · cfo** (revise vs clear on the Partner and Technical Proof Programme row) — **clear.** The
door closes by Month 5 for Month 6 work, the cadence names both cards and their months, and the
budget frame column is filled; nothing here is unfundable.

**11 · competitor** (revise vs clear on the Advocacy Enablement Push row) — **clear.** An
ownership row hands a competitor nothing.

## 12–14. Open — waiting on a person

Both upstream-trace seats agreed on a defect here (recorded), citing the toolkit's Month 12 close
row; the close is owned by name (Kamen Kamenov) and needs no door, so that agreed defect reads as a
seat misread — it stays recorded, as the machinery requires, and is flagged below.

**12 · cfo** (clear vs revise on the door list) — **revise.** The same role-as-assigner weakness
as 9, seen from the funder's side: nine doors, none openable by a named person.

**13 · employee-insider** (clear vs revise on "Who owns each roadmap element? — drafted above; no
owner has approved" beside the approve door) — **revise.** The generic round-1 line now
contradicts the door under it, which names who approves and by when. Repair: drop the generic line;
the approve door and the owner doors say everything it said.

**14 · competitor** (clear vs revise on the same two lines) — **clear.** Positioning is untouched.

## 15–16. Client Style Note applied

**15 · journalist** and **16 · employee-insider** (revise vs clear on "signature do words") —
**clear, both**, as Kamen ruled the identical line on version 1 and version 2. Note: this time the
two competitor seats agreed the line is a defect (recorded) — the do-words are the category's
generic vocabulary. That agreed defect stands in the register whatever these two rulings say.

## The four agreed defects and the one refusal (no ruling needed)

| cell | what the seats located |
| --- | --- |
| proof-building order · upstream-trace | the paragraph never relates UP's generic order to the one-pager's practical order for this engagement |
| ownership · employee-insider | doors name roles ("United Partners project lead", "The B Team delivery owner"), not people |
| open doors · upstream-trace | cites the toolkit's Month 12 close — owned by name on the page; reads as a seat misread, recorded as agreed |
| client style note · competitor | the do-words line is generic category vocabulary |
| senior craft story series · upstream-trace | **recording refusal**: one seat's revise cited a 23-character producer passage, under the 25-character rule; the cell is refused, not judged |

## Located weaknesses for a round 3 (candidate boundaries)

1. **Named assigners** — an unassigned door names a person the page already knows (the platform
   decider or a named owner), never a role (ownership ×2, open doors ×1: 3 cells + the agreed
   insider defect).
2. **Proof-building order tied to the calendar** — each stage names its cards and months, in the
   producer's practical order (proof-building ×2 + the agreed upstream defect: 3 cells).
3. **Retire the generic open-items line** once the derived doors exist (1 cell).

Seven located cells across three boundaries, down from fifteen across five. The style-note
competitor defect and the Month-12 misread stay recorded and are not proposed as boundaries.
